  "body": "What happens: the program's length unit is decided by the loaded machine file (`kinematics.default.units.length`, parsed in `crates/post/src/machine/parse.rs` into `machine.units`). Only the three Tormach posts read it and write G20 / G21. Carvera refuses an inch machine and writes G21; Heidenhain writes G71; the Fanuc lathe post hard-codes G21. The other mill posts - `assets/posts/fanuc.cps`, `haas.cps`, `mazak.cps`, `sinumerik.cps`, `doosan-dnm.cps` - write no unit word at all (no G21 / G20, no G71 / G710 on Sinumerik) and never read `machine.units`.\n\nWhy it matters (Prime Directive): these posts always write millimetre numbers. On a control whose modal unit is inch (the usual power-on state of a Haas or Fanuc in an inch shop, or the state a previous inch program left), every coordinate is read as inches: moves are 25.4x too long. And a machine file that states inch gets a millimetre program with no diagnostic.\n\nExpected:\n- Each of these posts states the program's unit in its safety block (G21, or the dialect's own word - Sinumerik G71 / G710), before any motion.\n- A machine file that states inch is either honoured (inch output: lengths / 25.4 at four decimals, feeds converted, G20) or refused by name at post time - never posted in mm silently. Which of the two per post is a decision to record in `docs/decisions/`.\n- The document's Units choice (Stock page radio, `SetUnits`) stays display-only; it must not be a second source for the posted unit.\n\nEvidence: `grep -n \"G21\\|G20\\|G71\\|machine.units\" assets/posts/{fanuc,haas,mazak,sinumerik,doosan-dnm}.cps` prints nothing; compare `assets/posts/tormach.cps:189-194`. Found while answering the maintainer's question \"what sets the length unit for post outputs\" (2026-11-11).\n\nRelated: the UI shows no place where the posted unit is stated (it is only a row in the machine sheet); a line in the post dialog naming the program's unit and where it comes from would answer that question in the app - file separately if wanted.\n\nDone means: golden programs of the five posts carry the unit word in the opening block; a test per post pins it; an inch machine file through each post is either pinned as inch output or pinned as a named refusal.\n",
